## 2. Licensing Strategy: Which `LICENSE` Fits Lucid Tech Best?

Because your React storefront references an **MIT reference gateway** while you are simultaneously launching commercial B2B SaaS tiers ($599–$6,400/month), there are three legitimate licensing approaches—each with distinct trade-offs[span_13](start_span)[span_13](end_span):

| Option | Legal Friction for Pilots | Patent & Brand Protection | Commercial Moat | When to Choose It |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Option A: MIT License (Recommended for Pilot Launch)** | Lowest (instant trust; zero counsel review needed) | Basic (no explicit patent grant) | Relies on managed hosting, custom rules, and SLAs | Best right now while closing your 5 Founding Partners (matches your storefront's MIT reference)[span_14](start_span)[span_14](end_span). |
| **Option B: Apache License 2.0** | Very low (standard enterprise permissive license) | Strong (explicit patent grant + trademark exclusion) | Same open-core model, stronger legal hygiene | Best if larger carriers or TMS vendors ask for formal patent-indemnity clarity before adopting the open core. |
| **Option C: Dual-License (MIT Core + Commercial Pro)** | Medium (requires clear boundary between repos/folders) | Strongest | High (proprietary multi-tenant billing, managed connectors, and compliance binders stay closed) | Best once you scale past 5+ paying clients and want a private `lucid-truth-enterprise` repo for paid add-ons. |

---

